      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "2015 BMJ Publishing Group Ltd.",
        "AbstractText": "A 61-year-old man presented with a 1-month history of breathlessness, chest pain and lethargy. He had been taking adalimumab for ankylosing spondylitis for 2\u2005years. Pleural and pericardial effusions were both found. A video-assisted thorascopic (VATS) pleural and lung biopsy were performed. The pleural pathology showed eosinophils, acute inflammatory cells and lymphoid aggregates. The patient was positive for antinuclear, antidouble-stranded and antihistone antibodies consistent with drug-induced lupus due to adalimumab. His serositis resolved on withdrawal of the drug. Drug-induced lupus can occur as a consequence of anti-TNF-α agents from induction of autoimmunity in a predisposed host."
      },
